{# Fil d'Ariane — visible only when the site runs as an installed PWA
   (display-mode: standalone). Visibility is 100% CSS (see app.css's
   .breadcrumb-bar rule) — no server flag, no JS detection, since this is a
   navigation convenience, never a security boundary (SECURITY §3, same
   principle as menu visibility).

   The home icon is always hard-coded to "/", never sourced from config.
   Everything past it comes from the current route's optional `breadcrumb`
   declaration (Core\Http\Router::addRoute()'s 6th argument for core routes,
   module.json's `routes[].breadcrumb` for module routes) — a route that
   declares none simply stops the trail at the home icon. `parents` are
   plain-text ancestor labels, never links (most menu categories have no
   single landing page). `breadcrumb_current`, when the controller passes
   it, replaces the route's static `label` for a dynamic page title (a
   member's name, an article title...). #}
<nav class="breadcrumb-bar border-bottom bg-light px-3 py-2" aria-label="Fil d'Ariane">
    <ol class="breadcrumb mb-0 small align-items-center">
        <li class="breadcrumb-item">
            <a href="/" class="text-decoration-none" aria-label="Accueil">
                <i class="bi bi-house-door"></i>
            </a>
        </li>
        {% set breadcrumb = route_breadcrumb|default(null) %}
        {% if breadcrumb is not null and current_path|default('/') != '/' %}
            {% for parent in breadcrumb.parents %}
                <li class="breadcrumb-item text-body-secondary">{{ parent }}</li>
            {% endfor %}
            <li class="breadcrumb-item active text-truncate" aria-current="page">
                {{ breadcrumb_current|default(breadcrumb.label) }}
            </li>
        {% endif %}
    </ol>
</nav>
